    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Power_Transmission_Network\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 300)\n\n    # Problem Data\n    n_nodes = 5\n    nodes = list(range(n_nodes))\n    n_generators = 2\n\n    # Node coordinates (x, y) in km\n    node_coords = [(20, 72), (56, 37), (27, 84), (70, 88), (30, 66)]\n\n    # Node types\n    node_types = ['generator', 'generator', 'consumer', 'consumer', 'consumer']\n\n    # Power demands and generator capacities (MW)\n    power_demands = [0.0, 0.0, 41.0, 11.0, 10.0]\n    generator_capacities = [148.0, 138.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]\n\n    # Wire physical properties (INDUCED PARAMETERS)\n    resistivity = 1.68e-08  # Ohm-meter\n    wire_area_mm2 = 500.0  # mm^2\n    wire_area_m2 = wire_area_mm2 * 1e-6  # Convert to m^2\n\n    # Operating parameters\n    operating_voltage = 220.0  # kV\n    line_capacity = 100.0  # MW\n    build_cost_per_km = 100000.0  # $/km\n    loss_cost_rate = 50.0  # $/MWh equivalent\n\n    # COMPUTE distance and resistance matrices (INDUCED COSTS)\n    # Using Ohm's Law: R = resistivity * length / area\n    def compute_distance_km(coord1, coord2):\n        return math.sqrt((coord1[0] - coord2[0])**2 + (coord1[1] - coord2[1])**2)\n\n    def compute_resistance(dist_km):\n        dist_m = dist_km * 1000  # Convert km to m\n        return resistivity * dist_m / wire_area_m2\n\n    distances = {}  # km\n    resistances = {}  # Ohm\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                dist = compute_distance_km(node_coords[i], node_coords[j])\n                distances[(i, j)] = dist\n                resistances[(i, j)] = compute_resistance(dist)\n\n    # Create edge list (undirected, i < j)\n    edges = [(i, j) for i in nodes for j in nodes if i < j]\n\n    # Decision Variables\n    # Build_i_j: Binary - whether to build line between nodes i and j\n    build = {}\n    for (i, j) in edges:\n        build[(i, j)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Build_{i}_{j}\")\n\n    # Flow_i_j: Continuous - power flow from node i to j (MW)\n    flow = {}\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                flow[(i, j)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Flow_{i}_{j}\")\n\n    # Helper function to get build variable (handles i > j case)\n    def get_build(i, j):\n        if i < j:\n            return build[(i, j)]\n        else:\n            return build[(j, i)]\n\n    # Objective: Minimize construction cost + operational transmission cost\n    # Cost factor: converts resistance to cost coefficient\n    # Cost = LossRate * (R / V^2) * P^2\n    # factor = LossRate / V^2\n    loss_factor = loss_cost_rate / (operating_voltage ** 2)\n\n    # Construction costs (based on distance)\n    obj = quicksum(distances[(i, j)] * build_cost_per_km * build[(i, j)] for (i, j) in edges)\n\n    # Operational costs: Quadratic (I^2*R losses)\n    # Note: Gurobi supports quadratic objectives directly\n    obj += quicksum(\n        resistances[(i, j)] * loss_factor * flow[(i, j)] * flow[(i, j)]\n        for i in nodes for j in nodes if i != j\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Flow conservation constraints\n    for node in nodes:\n        inflow = quicksum(flow[(other, node)] for other in nodes if other != node)\n        outflow = quicksum(flow[(node, other)] for other in nodes if other != node)\n        \n        if node_types[node] == 'generator':\n            # Generator: net outflow (generation) <= capacity\n            # Rewritten: inflow - outflow >= -capacity\n            m.addConstr(\n                inflow - outflow >= -generator_capacities[node],\n                name=f\"Gen_Cap_{node}\"\n            )\n        else:\n            # Consumer: net inflow >= demand\n            m.addConstr(\n                inflow - outflow >= power_demands[node],\n                name=f\"Demand_{node}\"\n            )\n\n    # Capacity constraints: Flow <= line_capacity * Build\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    flow[(i, j)] <= line_capacity * get_build(i, j),\n                    name=f\"Cap_{i}_{j}\"\n                )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Planning approach for a five-node transmission build-out with quadratic loss costs\n\nWe are planning a new transmission network connecting two generator sites and three consumer sites (five nodes in total). The locations for all nodes are defined by map coordinates in Node Coordinates (km):\n  - N0 (generator): (20, 72)\n  - N1 (generator): (56, 37)\n  - N2 (consumer): (27, 84)\n  - N3 (consumer): (70, 88)\n  - N4 (consumer): (30, 66), and each location\u2019s role (generator or consumer) is identified in ['generator', 'generator', 'consumer', 'consumer', 'consumer']. Consumer power requirements are summarized in Consumer demands: N2: 41.0 MW, N3: 11.0 MW, N4: 10.0 MW, while generator output limits are specified in Generator capacities: N0: 148.0 MW, N1: 138.0 MW.\n\nWe have no predefined corridors; we may build lines between any pair of nodes. Power can only flow over lines that we choose to build. Each built line is bidirectional and limited by its rating in either direction, with the per-line transfer capability set by 100.0 MW. Every consumer must receive at least its stated demand, and each generator must remain within its maximum output. Delivering surplus to a consumer is acceptable if it arises from the chosen topology and power routing.\n\nAll spans will use copper conductors. The wire material specification is provided in 1.68e-08 \u03a9\u00b7m (copper), and the selected conductor cross-sectional area is given in 500.0 mm\u00b2. Together with span length, these define the resistance of each line segment; longer runs and smaller cross-sections increase resistance, which in turn drives higher heating when current flows.\n\nWe will operate the network at 220.0 kV. Because current is power divided by voltage, operating at higher voltage reduces current for a given power transfer and therefore reduces resistive heating. These heating losses follow a square law: if the power flow on a line doubles, the associated resistive losses increase by roughly four times. In practical terms, loss on a line is proportional to its resistance times the square of (power divided by the operating voltage). This physics is central to our planning; we will not rely on linear approximations, since they understate the penalty of high flows and can bias the design toward unrealistic operating points.\n\nOur total cost has two components:\n\n- Construction cost: For each built span, we pay a distance-based cost using $100,000/km multiplied by the length between the connected nodes. A sparser network reduces this upfront spend but can concentrate flows, which may raise losses significantly.\n\n- Operational heating cost: We monetize resistive energy losses using $50.0/MWh. The cost on each span grows with its resistance and the square of the power flow relative to the operating voltage. This means that pushing a little more power through a line can become disproportionately expensive, while distributing transfers across multiple paths or operating at higher voltage can reduce the current and lower costs through the 1/V^2 effect.\n\nFor planning purposes, we treat power flow as conserved at each node: the inflows and outflows across the network must balance without deducting losses at the nodes themselves. The heating losses are accounted for as a separate financial penalty, not as a physical subtraction from deliveries. This simplifies system accounting while preserving the real physics in the cost model.\n\nThe objective is to choose which lines to build and how to route power so that the combined total of construction costs and square-law heating costs is minimized, while meeting all consumer demands and respecting generator and line limits. The core engineering trade-off is straightforward to articulate:\n\n- Longer lines add distance-based construction cost, and their greater length raises resistance.\n- Concentrating large transfers on a few paths lowers build cost but drives up I^2R heating, with costs growing quadratically as flows increase.\n- Spreading transfers or adding alternative paths can reduce peak currents and losses, but at the expense of additional line length and associated build cost.\n- Higher operating voltage reduces current for the same power, providing a strong lever to mitigate loss costs.\n\nGiven the five-node geography and the provided demand and generation envelopes, we will evaluate topologies that best balance these effects. The intent is to produce a design that reliably serves all consumers at minimum total cost, using the correct physics-based loss model rather than linear surrogates, and making transparent the trade-offs between construction spending and ongoing heating costs.",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Planning approach for a five-node transmission build-out with quadratic loss costs\n\nWe are planning a new transmission network connecting two generator sites and three consumer sites (five nodes in total). The locations for all nodes are defined by map coordinates in {NODE_COORDINATES}, and each location\u2019s role (generator or consumer) is identified in {NODE_TYPES}. Consumer power requirements are summarized in {POWER_DEMANDS}, while generator output limits are specified in {GENERATOR_CAPACITIES}.\n\nWe have no predefined corridors; we may build lines between any pair of nodes. Power can only flow over lines that we choose to build. Each built line is bidirectional and limited by its rating in either direction, with the per-line transfer capability set by {LINE_CAPACITY}. Every consumer must receive at least its stated demand, and each generator must remain within its maximum output. Delivering surplus to a consumer is acceptable if it arises from the chosen topology and power routing.\n\nAll spans will use copper conductors. The wire material specification is provided in {WIRE_RESISTIVITY}, and the selected conductor cross-sectional area is given in {WIRE_CROSS_SECTION}. Together with span length, these define the resistance of each line segment; longer runs and smaller cross-sections increase resistance, which in turn drives higher heating when current flows.\n\nWe will operate the network at {OPERATING_VOLTAGE}. Because current is power divided by voltage, operating at higher voltage reduces current for a given power transfer and therefore reduces resistive heating. These heating losses follow a square law: if the power flow on a line doubles, the associated resistive losses increase by roughly four times. In practical terms, loss on a line is proportional to its resistance times the square of (power divided by the operating voltage). This physics is central to our planning; we will not rely on linear approximations, since they understate the penalty of high flows and can bias the design toward unrealistic operating points.\n\nOur total cost has two components:\n\n- Construction cost: For each built span, we pay a distance-based cost using {BUILD_COST_PER_KM} multiplied by the length between the connected nodes. A sparser network reduces this upfront spend but can concentrate flows, which may raise losses significantly.\n\n- Operational heating cost: We monetize resistive energy losses using {LOSS_COST_RATE}. The cost on each span grows with its resistance and the square of the power flow relative to the operating voltage. This means that pushing a little more power through a line can become disproportionately expensive, while distributing transfers across multiple paths or operating at higher voltage can reduce the current and lower costs through the 1/V^2 effect.\n\nFor planning purposes, we treat power flow as conserved at each node: the inflows and outflows across the network must balance without deducting losses at the nodes themselves. The heating losses are accounted for as a separate financial penalty, not as a physical subtraction from deliveries. This simplifies system accounting while preserving the real physics in the cost model.\n\nThe objective is to choose which lines to build and how to route power so that the combined total of construction costs and square-law heating costs is minimized, while meeting all consumer demands and respecting generator and line limits. The core engineering trade-off is straightforward to articulate:\n\n- Longer lines add distance-based construction cost, and their greater length raises resistance.\n- Concentrating large transfers on a few paths lowers build cost but drives up I^2R heating, with costs growing quadratically as flows increase.\n- Spreading transfers or adding alternative paths can reduce peak currents and losses, but at the expense of additional line length and associated build cost.\n- Higher operating voltage reduces current for the same power, providing a strong lever to mitigate loss costs.\n\nGiven the five-node geography and the provided demand and generation envelopes, we will evaluate topologies that best balance these effects. The intent is to produce a design that reliably serves all consumers at minimum total cost, using the correct physics-based loss model rather than linear surrogates, and making transparent the trade-offs between construction spending and ongoing heating costs."
}